    {
      "requirement_id": "008-settings-cli:AC-001",
      "disposition": "covered",
      "rationale": "The existing-executable settings group and preserved legacy flags have explicit console, startup side-effect and release-latency outcomes; these are independently observable and consistent with the native evidence constitution.",
      "source_citations": [
        {
          "section_id": ".specs/features/008-settings-cli/spec.md:L206-L210",
          "excerpt": "**Criterion:** `handy settings` is a subcommand group of the existing `handy` executable. Existing flags (`--toggle-transcription`, `--toggle-post-process`, `--cancel`, `--start-hidden`, `--no-tray`, `--debug`, `--transcribe-file`, `--model`, `--device-index`, `--list-devices`, `--list-models`, `--repeat`, `--json`) keep their behavior; `--json` after `settings` belongs to the subcommand. It works on macOS, Windows and Linux; on Windows, output appears in the console the command was started from. A `handy settings` invocation never shows a window, a tray icon or loads a model, never triggers the single-instance \"show window\" behavior, and completes reads within 2 seconds on an Apple M1 Mac in a release build."
        }
      ],
      "plan_citations": [],
      "searched_plan_sections": []
    },
    {
      "requirement_id": "008-settings-cli:AC-002",
      "disposition": "covered",
      "rationale": "Generic read/discovery/scalar writes define all type and refusal cases, both JSON placements, secret presence projection and no store creation. This is a complete observable command contract.",
      "source_citations": [
        {
          "section_id": ".specs/features/008-settings-cli/spec.md:L211-L215",
          "excerpt": "**Criterion:** `handy settings get [KEY]` returns all non-secret settings or one key; `handy settings keys` lists settable keys with type, allowed values or range and read-only flag; `handy settings set KEY VALUE` changes one scalar setting. Secret values (API keys) are never printed; reads report only their presence. Unknown keys return `not_found`. `set` parses VALUE with the key's descriptor type (boolean `true`/`false`, integer, enumeration value, string). Generic `set` on a list key returns `validation_failed` naming the domain command to use; on a secret or read-only key it returns `validation_failed` stating that the key is changed in the settings UI. A top-level `--json` before `settings` is accepted as equivalent to `--json` after it. A headless read never creates the settings store."
        }
      ],
      "plan_citations": [],
      "searched_plan_sections": []
    },
    {
      "requirement_id": "008-settings-cli:AC-003",
      "disposition": "covered",
      "rationale": "One backend validation authority is required for every UI/CLI write, including replacement of invalid-to-default coercion. Equal English errors and unchanged state on rejection are testable invariants.",
      "source_citations": [
        {
          "section_id": ".specs/features/008-settings-cli/spec.md:L216-L220",
          "excerpt": "**Criterion:** Every write goes through the same backend validation functions as the corresponding UI command, so a value the UI rejects is rejected by the CLI with the same error code and English message, and vice versa; the UI may show that message translated. UI commands that today replace an invalid value by a default (for example an unknown paste method) reject it instead. A rejected write changes nothing."
        }
      ],
      "plan_citations": [],
      "searched_plan_sections": []
    },
    {
      "requirement_id": "008-settings-cli:AC-004",
      "disposition": "covered",
      "rationale": "Every effective committed write has one persisted revision increment; no-op/read do not. Cross-process serialization with fresh reread and expected-revision conflict details directly prevent lost updates.",
      "source_citations": [
        {
          "section_id": ".specs/features/008-settings-cli/spec.md:L221-L225",
          "excerpt": "**Criterion:** Settings carry a persisted revision number returned by every read and incremented by 1 on every committed write from any surface, including writes made by Handy itself; a write that changes nothing and a read do not increment it. Writes accept `--expected-revision N`; a mismatch returns `revision_conflict` with the current revision in `error.details.current_revision` and writes nothing. All writers (UI commands, a running instance and a headless CLI process) are serialized by one cross-process settings lock, and each writer re-reads the stored settings under that lock before applying its change, so a write without expected revision applies to the latest settings and never discards fields changed by another writer."
        }
      ],
      "plan_citations": [],
      "searched_plan_sections": []
    },
    {
      "requirement_id": "008-settings-cli:AC-005",
      "disposition": "covered",
      "rationale": "The live/new-instance channel, private user scope, deadline/unknown outcome, legacy refusal and offline same-service branch are explicit. It requires no unsupported replay and no network listener.",
      "source_citations": [
        {
          "section_id": ".specs/features/008-settings-cli/spec.md:L226-L230",
          "excerpt": "**Criterion:** When a Handy instance of the same user and settings store is running, the CLI sends the operation to it over a local channel accessible only to that user and waits up to 5 seconds for its answer; the instance applies it with its live state, re-registers affected shortcuts and notifies its UI. If no answer arrives in time, the CLI returns `unavailable` with `error.details.outcome` set to `unknown`, and agents must read again before retrying. If a running Handy without this channel (an older version) is detected, the CLI returns `unavailable` and never writes the store itself. When none is running, the CLI applies the operation to the settings store itself under the same lock and validation. Operations that need live state (for example the current destination) return `unavailable` without a running instance. No network listener and no MCP server are introduced."
        }
      ],
      "plan_citations": [],
      "searched_plan_sections": []
